. local charttitle "Figure R4-03. The mix of firms in the `covid_year' and `covid_end' files against normal years: chang
> e in the share of firms and of workers by size, imports, foreign ownership and sector"

. local ntext "Notes: Each row = a group's share of all firms (a) or of all workers (b) in an annual file. Dot = change 
> from the `covid_year' file to the `covid_end' file, in percentage points; hollow = the same change from `placebo_year'
>  to `placebo_end'; gray bar = lowest to highest change over the normal-years spans (starting files `nb_first' to `nb_l
> ast', each compared with the file three years on). (c) splits the dot in (a) into three parts that add up to it: `covi
> d_year' firms not in the `covid_end' file (`n_left'), compared with the firms in both files in `covid_year'; `covid_en
> d' firms not in the `covid_year' file (`n_came'), compared with the firms in both files in `covid_end'; and the change
>  within the firms in both files (`n_both'), e.g. shrinking below 20 workers or, for sectors, a changed sector code. Sh
> ares among firms with a known answer; sectors with under 30 firms in `covid_year' or `covid_end' are pooled; sector co
> des are not checked across years. Exporters are not shown: the `covid_end' file records no non-exporters. Gray: the re
> corded importer share jumps in 2017 and falls in `covid_end', likely a recording change. Whole annual files, not sampl
> es, so no sampling interval; the gray bar shows normal movement. Shows how the mix changed, not the effect of COVID; l
> eft the files is not proof of closure. (a) and (b) use no firm links; (c) does. Firm links are fictional in the develo
> pment data, so who stays, leaves or comes back means nothing here; read these results on the real BPS files."

. local notelines ""

. local line ""

. foreach w of local ntext {
  2.     if ustrlen("`line' `w'") > 130 & "`line'" != "" {
  3.         local notelines `"`notelines' "`line'""'
  4.         local line "`w'"
  5.     }
  6.     else local line = strtrim("`line' `w'")
  7. }

. local notelines `"`notelines' "`line'""'

. display `"   note lines: `notelines'"'
   note lines:  "Notes: Each row = a group's share of all firms (a) or of all workers (b) in an annual file. Dot = chang
> e from the 2019 file to the" "2022 file, in percentage points; hollow = the same change from 2012 to 2015; gray bar = 
> lowest to highest change over the" "normal-years spans (starting files 2010 to 2012, each compared with the file three
>  years on). (c) splits the dot in (a) into three" "parts that add up to it: 2019 firms not in the 2022 file (1,329), c
> ompared with the firms in both files in 2019; 2022 firms not in" "the 2019 file (1,499), compared with the firms in bo
> th files in 2022; and the change within the firms in both files (1,678), e.g." "shrinking below 20 workers or, for sec
> tors, a changed sector code. Shares among firms with a known answer; sectors with under 30" "firms in 2019 or 2022 are
>  pooled; sector codes are not checked across years. Exporters are not shown: the 2022 file records no" "non-exporters.
>  Gray: the recorded importer share jumps in 2017 and falls in 2022, likely a recording change. Whole annual files," "n
> ot samples, so no sampling interval; the gray bar shows normal movement. Shows how the mix changed, not the effect of 
> COVID; left" "the files is not proof of closure. (a) and (b) use no firm links; (c) does. Firm links are fictional in 
> the development data, so" "who stays, leaves or comes back means nothing here; read these results on the real BPS file
> s."

